- op: discuss  # 61db
  at: '2026-04-18T01:38:07Z'  # 61db
  by: agent  # 61db
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 61db
  clock: 4  # 61db
  nonce: 61db  # 61db
  message: "Incorporating WI-zakub lessons into this item's scope:\n\n1. Open question resolved — span format is line/col (UTF-8 column units), not byte offsets. rust.py's stable_id derivation already uses a line/col Span per ADR-0014, so byte-offset conversion is NOT a concern; the column-unit matter only becomes load-bearing when UTF-8 multi-byte characters appear in identifiers (rare in Rust source). Treat column units as UTF-8 code-units and document that assumption near the mapping helper.\n\n2. Scope expansion for trait-impl symbols — WI-zakub discovered rust-analyzer leaves SCIP Relationship empty; trait-impl information is carried only in the symbol descriptor itself (e.g., \"rust-analyzer cargo crate_name version impl#[T][Trait]method()\"). This changes the mapping strategy:\n   - The stable_id shared prefix must be computed from the SCIP Occurrence's enclosing_range (the source-span of the concrete function definition), NOT from the descriptor — that is the only anchor rust.py can also observe.\n   - The descriptor's \"impl#[T][Trait]\" prefix produces SCIP-only suffix information (resolved trait dispatch). When two rust.py runs see `fn foo` inside `impl Trait for T` vs inside `impl OtherTrait for T` at different spans, they already get distinct stable_ids via span disambiguation. The SCIP mapping should preserve that — same span → same shared stable_id prefix, trait info goes in the suffix.\n   - Add a unit test: take a single impl block with two trait impls in the same .rs file and assert that SCIP-derived stable_ids match rust.py-derived stable_ids for both method definitions.\n\n3. Macro-expanded items are NOT present in the SCIP index (WI-zakub §2) — so the mapping function never needs to dedupe a \"rust.py sees it, SCIP sees it too\" pair for macro-generated code. The scope therefore excludes proc-macro-generated symbols; those remain handled by the existing per-macro FFI linkers (wasm_bindgen, napi, pyO3). Document this explicitly in the helper's docstring so future maintainers don't waste time trying to cover that case.\n\n4. Opt-in posture — because rust-analyzer SCIP is 10x+ slower than tree-sitter at every realistic size (22s on ripgrep, per WI-zakub), the stable_id helper's test fixtures should use recorded SCIP files, not live rust-analyzer invocations, to keep CI wall-time manageable. The live-invocation path stays behind the opt-in flag tracked by WI-nohah.\n\n5. Test inventory grows to: (a) golden file with two concrete-method impls in one file to verify trait-resolution suffix handling, (b) rust.py-vs-SCIP parity on a single-file corpus, (c) one multi-file crate sampled from the WI-zakub ripgrep fixture to verify scaling behavior.\n\nNo change to the item's status (still todo_soft, P2, blocks WI-duzul) — these are scope refinements, not scope additions."  # 61db
